1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,663
DispatcherServlet can route only if
something exists to route *to*.
Controllers are that something: thin Java
types whose methods claim URLs and
translate HTTP into harbor-domain calls.

2
00:00:11,783 --> 00:00:23,088
Without a convention, every team invents
its own router for luggage: a giant
servlet with a switch on the path, a hand-
rolled map of strings to method handles,
or XML that points method names at URLs.

3
00:00:23,207 --> 00:00:34,320
Spring MVC’s answer is annotation-driven
controllers. You mark a class as a web
endpoint. You mark methods with HTTP verbs
and path patterns.

4
00:00:34,439 --> 00:00:44,759
At startup, RequestMappingHandlerMapping
reads those annotations and builds the
lookup table the dispatcher already uses.
Two stereotypes show up constantly.

5
00:00:44,879 --> 00:00:50,303
@Controller is the classic stereotype for
MVC that often returns a view name.

6
00:00:50,423 --> 00:01:04,751
@RestController is @Controller plus
@ResponseBody on the type: return values
are written to the response body,
typically as JSON, not resolved as view
names. For a luggage API, @RestController
is the everyday choice.

7
00:01:04,871 --> 00:01:20,135
Look at this java for a moment. Read that
against a live request. Client sends GET
/luggage/TAG-8841. Mapping matches class
path /luggage plus method path /{tag} with
verb GET.

8
00:01:20,255 --> 00:01:39,335
The adapter binds path variable tag to
TAG-8841. Your method runs. The return
value is serialized. Status defaults to
200 unless you change it. No XML. No
manual registration of the method with the
servlet.

9
00:01:39,455 --> 00:01:53,039
Method mapping annotations are shortcuts
over @RequestMapping. @GetMapping,
@PostMapping, @PutMapping, @PatchMapping,
and @DeleteMapping fix the HTTP method so
the declaration reads like the contract.

10
00:01:53,159 --> 00:02:05,759
Path variables use {name} in the pattern
and @PathVariable on the parameter. Query
parameters use @RequestParam. Headers and
cookies have their own annotations when
you need them.

11
00:02:05,879 --> 00:02:15,623
Prefer constructor injection for the
lookup collaborator so the controller
stays easy to test with a fake
LuggageLookup. Keep controllers thin on
purpose.

12
00:02:15,743 --> 00:02:27,935
The method should bind input, call one use
case, and shape the HTTP result. Tag
status rules belong in the domain service.
Persistence belongs in repositories.

13
00:02:28,055 --> 00:02:40,631
When a luggage controller grows if trees
for customs holds and berth assignments,
you are no longer writing a translation
layer — you are hiding a service inside a
web adapter. Return types are part of the
mapping story.

14
00:02:40,751 --> 00:02:52,751
A plain object becomes the response body
under @RestController. ResponseEntity<T>
lets you set status and headers — useful
when a missing tag should be 404 rather
than an empty 200.

15
00:02:52,871 --> 00:03:05,255
void with a status annotation can work for
no-content responses. For HTML MVC, a
String view name plus a Model parameter is
still valid — same dispatcher, different
adapter outcome.

16
00:03:05,375 --> 00:03:13,007
Component scanning must see the
controller: put it under the Boot
application’s package tree or declare an
explicit @ComponentScan.

17
00:03:13,127 --> 00:03:23,111
If the class is invisible to the context,
the mapping never exists, and the
dispatcher correctly answers 404. Testing
stays close to the type.

18
00:03:23,231 --> 00:03:29,207
A unit test can new up LuggageController
with a fake lookup and call
get("TAG-8841").

19
00:03:29,327 --> 00:03:39,263
A slice test with @WebMvcTest loads MVC
infrastructure without the full Boot
context and asserts that GET
/luggage/TAG-8841 returns JSON.

20
00:03:39,383 --> 00:03:45,407
Both styles reinforce the same idea: the
controller is an adapter, not the harbor
domain.

21
00:03:45,527 --> 00:03:58,223
@RestController and @Controller are not
interchangeable "as long as Jackson is on
the classpath." A plain @Controller method
returning an object without @ResponseBody
is treated as a view name or model dance,

22
00:03:58,343 --> 00:04:00,719
not a JSON body.

23
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:09,719
Stuffing every harbor path onto one god
controller also hurts: split by resource —
luggage, berths, manifests — so mappings
stay discoverable.

24
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:18,120
And treating path variables as trusted
domain ids without checking existence is
how you leak 500s instead of deliberate
404s.

25
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:27,336
You now know what sits at the end of the
dispatcher pipeline: annotated methods
that claim verbs and paths, with
/luggage/{tag} as the mental model.

26
00:04:27,456 --> 00:04:38,855
Mapping a method still leaves a bigger
contract open — how those methods should
speak HTTP as an API: status codes,
resource shapes, and content types, rather
than merely returning a Java object.

27
00:04:38,975 --> 00:04:41,687
That contract is REST design.
